hi there steve here i'm back in  vancouver  uh no more sunshine palm springs desert  sunshine we're back in the rain country  hello let's not overdo it uh from here  on in the the weather in vancouver will  improve today i want to talk about  making language learning easy  remember if you enjoy these podcasts  please subscribe so  learning a language you're having to  overcome a lot of resistance  there is the resistance that everything  is strange the words are new the  structures are new you can't make out  what people are saying it seems like an  almost endless task because there's  grammar rules with exceptions and  there's so many words to learn and when  you go to speak of course you make  mistakes and so  you're fighting this this  fair amount of resistance so it's very  important to make things easy now let me  explain some of the ways that i think  language learning is made easier  obviously you want to start with  easier material easy content  i've said before you don't want to stay  there forever you want to as soon as  possible get into authentic and  compelling content because that interest  is also going to help you overcome the  resistance but when you start out you  have to deal with short material 30  seconds one minute long  where  you know i believe that the vocabulary  particularly the verbs the focus should  be on the most common verbs and so that  you get repetition and you see them  often that's why these mini stories  circular mini stories that we're  developing are so useful and i'm doing  it in greek and i'm enjoying it  because there's lots of repetition  and even in the questions they don't ask  you to analyze or  show that you understood the story  whatever give you the answer  john went to the store  question did john go to the store it  makes it easy  for example  i'm enjoying doing our activities at  link after every lesson  why because it's easier to do these and  we have four right now we have  flash cards  we have  dictation where you hear the  text-to-speech and you have to type it  out we have multiple choice  and we have sort of fill in the blank  closed tests  these are made easier by the fact that  you're doing these  on material that you just studied and  since the lessons particularly for my  level in greek the lessons are short  30 seconds a minute long and then you go  through these uh activities about the  words that you were just exposed to  and necessarily in these 25  uh  you know flash cards or dictation  whatever  some of the same material will be gone  over more than once and in particular if  at least i save a word and then i save a  phrase that includes that word  then in my activities i will come across  the word and the phrase so all of this  makes it easy i'm likely to get it right  the part of the problem with  massive decks of  of uh flash cards is  there's several problems but one is that  you have a greater likelihood of getting  it wrong and the more you can create  situations where we get it right  the better in my opinion  uh another problem with the flashcards  is that eventually i you have so many  words given that i have an hour or so to  spend on language learning every day i  don't want to spend it all on reviewing  these uh these uh you know my just on  vocabulary i also want to have this  enjoyable experience of um  you know reading and listening to an  interesting story so that again makes it  easy  um  you know even in terms of grammar the  first time you're confronted with  grammar it seems overwhelming and so in  my own case with greek i've ignored more  or less the grammar i've had a quick  peek at it but i've ignored it until i  have come across these different  situations call it for example in in  greek you know the the the you know  direct object and the genitive and all  these different forms and i'm able to  because a lot of grammar in fact is  redundant  i am able to make out the meaning of the  text  and i get used to certain phrases  uh and i've even gotten used to certain  tenses although not really known exactly  which tense they were  but then i i have downloaded i have on  my ipad a resource for greek grammar now  i go there and i look at these things  and they refer to things that i have  already experienced so that makes it  easier and i find that having reviewed  the grammar a little bit not with the  idea of forcing myself to learn it but  just kind of reviewed it a bit i go back  and i start noticing those things better  when i'm listening and reading so all of  this to my mind is an easier way of  learning rather than trying to force  yourself rack your brain try and  remember the  tables the declension tables conjugation  tables get tested on it get it wrong  i think as much as possible you want to  set people up for situations where they  get it right  because ultimately in language learning  uh again the three keys right one of  which is the third one which i'll get to  is more difficult to control but the  first one is attitude and the second one  is time so anything that discourages you  frustrates you  dampens your enthusiasm is bad for  language learning anything which  therefore makes you want to spend less  time with the language is bad for  language learning so the more difficult  the tasks for most people there are the  bears for punishment that's fine but for  many people making it easy means that  they're more likely to enjoy the  experience more likely to spend the time  now the third element i've always said  is the ability to notice but the ability  to notice develops with time if you give  yourself enough exposure to the language  if you're determined to try to notice  things  like now after reviewing the grammar i  start to notice you know the direct  object or the genitive  better than i did before in my greek so  that's just to say that and there are  probably all kinds of ways of making it  easy and i think these mini stories are  an excellent way  to get from sort of the beginner stage  through to a fairly high level  and um  the way i've designed these many stories  that i'm having someone write for us is  that each level will address different  concepts in the language and these will  be translated into as many languages as  possible and all language languages deal  with the the idea of past present future  they all deal with probability might  should maybe they deal with conditional  if  they deal with  you know contrast on the other hand  despite the fact that so all of these  concepts are common to all languages  handled differently in different  languages so we're going to have sort of  a common theme in each of the five  levels and we have members now at link  and other people here at my followers at  youtube who are volunteering to help us  translate we have  quite a number of languages already with  people including arabic and farsi and  and stuff we don't yet have anyone for  french  in fact i'll put a link to the forum at  link  where this is discussed and you'll see  the range of languages for which people  have already volunteered to help us  translate and record and i think these  mini stories are also a great way to  ease yourself into the language and the  more we make it easy  the more people are going to want to  learn and the more people will succeed  in their language learning and that's  all thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]  you
 

